SyncPlayer: master prompt for Google AI Studio (Build)
16 Nov 2026 · for Carter to paste into a new AI Studio Build project

How to use it
1. Open AI Studio, start a new Build project, and paste everything below the line as
   the first message. Attach nothing else: this file is complete alone.
2. It asks for the work in six stages. After each stage it stops and prints a results
   table. Check the preview, then say "next stage" or say what to change.
3. When a stage is done, download the code (or push it to GitHub) and tell me; I run
   the same measurements on it that produced the numbers in section 2.
Nothing in this file contains library content, titles, tags, names or keys.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

0. WHAT YOU ARE BUILDING

You are the lead product designer and front-end engineer for SyncPlayer, a Windows
desktop app (Electron) that plays a very large local video library and drives a
motorised device, The Handy, in time with each video from a "funscript" (a timed list
of positions). The app works and is used every day. Its engine stays. You are
replacing its entire interface.

Build a new interface as a React + TypeScript app that runs in your preview against a
stand-in back end, and that can be dropped into the real app unchanged, because it
talks to the real app through one fixed object, window.electronAPI (Appendix A,
section 10).

The owner's words for the current interface: "clunky", "even the minus and plus UI
size buttons". He is right, and section 2 has the measurements. The bar for the new
one: the best player of its kind. Calm, fast, obviously designed by someone with
taste, for one person who sits in it for hours and steers it with the mouse and the
keyboard (a gamepad is supported, but it is not how he usually drives it).
The video is the point. Everything else gets out of its way and comes back the moment
it is wanted.

You have freedom over layout, navigation, component design, type scale, spacing,
motion, and how waiting and errors are shown. If you have a better idea than
something in this prompt, do it and say why in NOTES.md.
You do not have freedom over: what the app can do (every existing control keeps a
home, Appendix B), the back-end contract (Appendix A), the content rules (section 3),
the house colours and fonts (section 7), and the acceptance tests (section 9).


1. THE STACK AND WHAT TO DELIVER

Fit into this stack exactly; the real app is built with it:
- React 19 function components, TypeScript (strict), built by Vite. Icons from
  lucide-react (1.5px stroke). No other runtime dependency: no state library, no
  router, no component kit, no animation library.
- Plain CSS files using CSS variables. NO Tailwind and no CSS-in-JS: the real app has
  no CSS framework and one will not be added. If your environment adds Tailwind to
  the project by default, leave it unused and say so in NOTES.md.
- No inline style={{...}} except for values that are genuinely computed at run time
  (a progress width, a playhead position). The current app has 411 inline styles;
  that is one of the things being fixed.
- No network calls of any kind from the interface (no fetch, no WebSocket, no
  external fonts, no CDN at run time, no analytics). Everything goes through
  window.electronAPI.

Files to produce
  src/tokens.css            every colour, size, radius, space and duration as a variable
  src/ui/                   the shared primitives (Button, IconButton, Field, Select,
                            Slider, RangeSlider, Switch, Tabs, Segmented, Menu,
                            Popover, Drawer, Panel, Tooltip, Notice, ProgressRow,
