1. Content. Neutral placeholders only: "clip_01.mp4", "Series A", "Creator 1",
   "Playlist 1", "Tag 1", "Folder A", "Pattern set A", "Persona A", "Voice 1",
   "Model 1", short everyday sentences. Plain grey blocks for every image, thumbnail
   and portrait. The preview video is a generated test pattern. No real names, no
   faces, no adult or violent content, no invented statistics.
2. Do not add content filters, warnings, disclaimers, consent boxes or age gates.
   Where the app shows text the owner has configured, render the text it is given,
   unchanged; in the mock, show a grey block labelled "existing text, unchanged".
3. Every existing control keeps a home and keeps working (Appendix B). Nothing is
   dropped, merged away, or hidden with no way to reach it. You decide where it lives.
4. New features: only the ones marked NEW in this prompt, plus at most five of your
   own where they clearly earn their place, listed in MAPPING.md. Anything that would
   need a change inside the app's engine goes in NOTES.md as an idea, not into code.
5. The device connection (key field, Connect, Stop, Disconnect) is the most used and
   most trusted part of the app. It stays one glance away at every window size, never
   behind more than one click, and never cut off. The key is a secret: masked unless
   the field has focus, never logged, never in a tooltip or title.
6. Errors are inline, next to their cause, and say what to do next. No alert(), no
   confirm(), no modal for an error.
7. No emoji. No glass blur, backdrop-filter, gradient or glow anywhere.
8. Keep every existing localStorage key and its meaning (Appendix A, section 9), so
   nothing the owner has set is lost. New settings get new keys starting "syncplayer_".
9. Never rename, wrap or extend window.electronAPI. Check a method exists before
   calling it. Catch every call.


4. THE SHAPE OF THE NEW INTERFACE

These are requirements on the result, not a layout to copy. Beat the fallback below
if you can.

The picture is the hero. In the main window the video is the largest thing at every
size. Nothing sits above it and pushes it down: the title, the script version and the
actions live in one slim row (at most 3.5rem tall) or over the picture on hover.

The library is a list first. With filters closed, the only things above the list are
one search field and one row holding: active-filter chips, sort, view switch and the
count. Filters open in a panel beside or over the list, never above it. Rows are one
fixed height (about 3.5rem in list view): thumbnail, title on one line with a middle
ellipsis, then one line of facts (length, resolution, strokes/min) that never wraps.

One device panel, one way to think about it: a three-way switch, Script / Script +
fill / Manual, with only the chosen mode's controls underneath. Whatever is set is
summed up in one plain line ("Script + fill · hand-made patterns · pace 100%") that
opens the detail. Expert controls are folded and show a count when something in them
differs from the default. The panel docks beside the picture; it never opens over it
unless the owner asks.

A live bar (from the owner, 16 Nov). He changes four things constantly while a video
plays: the stroke range, pace and speed, the script version, and fill or manual
motion. All four are always visible in one compact bar directly under or beside the
picture, each reachable in ONE press and each showing its key: range presets with a
two-handle range; pace and speed; version buttons 1 to 9; the three-way Script /
Script + fill / Manual switch. The same bar is what appears over the picture in
fullscreen. The full device panel holds everything else.

Four ways in (from the owner). He picks videos by browsing, by building a queue, by
letting the app choose, and by searching, all regularly. None may be buried. Browse
and search are the library itself; "Add to queue" is on every row and tile; "Pick
for me" is one labelled control holding Shuffle, Roulette and Action Mix.

Detaching must be obvious (from the owner). Asked where the app sits on his three
screens he said: "it's on one screen because you can't detach". The pop-out windows
exist today, but behind unlabelled buttons, and they open at awkward sizes, so he
has never used them. Therefore:
- every main region (picture, library, device panel) has a visible, labelled "Move
