5. THE INTERFACE-SIZE SYSTEM (the owner's biggest complaint; treat as a feature)

5.1 One scale
- A single variable, --ui-scale, sizes the whole interface. Set
    html { font-size: calc(16px * var(--ui-scale)); }
  and write every font size, control height, icon size, gap, padding, radius and
  panel width in rem (or in tokens defined in rem). No bare px for anything the owner
  reads or clicks. 1px hairlines are the only exception.
- No CSS zoom and no transform: scale() for sizing, anywhere.
- The video picture is never scaled by it.
- Steps: 0.8, 0.9, 1, 1.1, 1.25, 1.4, 1.6. The stored value (localStorage key
  "syncplayer_ui_scale", a number) may hold an older value such as 0.6 or 0.67; snap
  it to the nearest step when read and keep the key.
- Base text is 0.875rem (14px at step 1.0). Nothing the owner reads is ever under
  12px on screen at any step: use max(12px, ...) on the smallest sizes.
- Click targets: at least 2rem (32px at 1.0); playback and device controls at least
  2.5rem; never under 28px on screen at any step.

5.2 Bigger size must behave exactly like a smaller window
This is the rule that makes scaling work instead of breaking the layout.
- Lay regions out with container queries written in rem/em, not media queries in px.
  Then a window W x H at step s arranges itself exactly as a window (W/s) x (H/s)
  does at step 1.0. Call that the "effective size".
- Arrangements by effective width (use these or better ones):
    under 56rem     one region at a time: stage; library and device are drawers
    56 to 80rem     library column + stage; device is a drawer or a slim docked strip
    80 to 120rem    library + stage + device panel, all docked
    over 120rem     as above, with optional "use the side space" (queue or device
                    seated beside a 16:9 picture on an ultrawide screen)
- By effective height: under 36rem the live motion strip folds to a thin line and
  the library's sort row folds into its menu.
- If the effective size falls under 50rem x 28rem the layout still works (everything
  reachable, panels scroll inside themselves) and the size control shows one quiet
  line: "Too large for this window".

5.3 The size control (replaces the A- and A+ buttons)
- It lives in the top bar at the far right, in a spot that does not move when the
  size changes. Closed, it shows the current size as a number: "100%".
- Open, it is a small popover anchored to that spot with: a minus button, a plus
  button, a stepped slider with seven stops, the number, "Reset to 100%", and a
  second row for the overlay size (5.5).
- While the popover is open its own position and size are frozen in screen pixels:
  the minus and plus buttons stay under the pointer however many times they are
  pressed. The rest of the window resizes live behind it.
- Dragging the slider previews live and commits on release. Esc puts back the size
  that was set when the popover opened.
- Ctrl + / Ctrl - / Ctrl 0 work everywhere, as today, and briefly show the number in
  the same top-bar spot.
- Appendix B keys lib-08 and lib-09 are the minus and plus buttons in this popover.

5.4 Per screen (NEW)
- The owner moves windows between screens with different scaling. Remember a size
  per screen: new key "syncplayer_ui_scale_by_screen", an object keyed by
  "<width>x<height>@<devicePixelRatio>" of the screen the window is on. When the
  window lands on another screen (listen for a devicePixelRatio or screen size
  change), apply that screen's value, or the global value if it has none.
- Every window kind (main, library, device, keys, chat, assistant controls, pop-out
  player) keeps its own value the same way, under its own key.

5.5 Overlay size (NEW)
- A second variable, --overlay-scale (steps 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2), sizes everything drawn
  over the picture in fullscreen and in the pop-out player, because that is read
  from further away. New key "syncplayer_overlay_scale".

5.6 Fullscreen and windows
- Fullscreen is its own layout: picture edge to edge on true black; inside a 4% safe
  margin the scrub heatmap, the live motion strip, the quick device deck (movable,
  remembers its place), title, clock and "up next". All of it appears on pointer
  move, key or pad press and fades after a few seconds. Nothing in it needs a small
  mouse target.
- Each window kind is complete alone, never scrolls sideways at any step, shows
  "waiting for the main window" until its first state message arrives, and has a
  clear "bring back" control. Where a panel has been popped out, its docked place
  shows one quiet line ("Device panel is in its own window · Bring back").
- The pop-out player is a bare picture: no frame, drag anywhere to move, controls
  only on hover, double-click for fullscreen, its own fit choice.
- OLED mode (NEW, in Display settings): surfaces around the video go to true black,
  nothing bright stays still during playback, the scrub bar dims and then hides.


