Not generated-looking. A reviewer will reject work that shows: a grid of equal cards
and stat tiles; rounded box inside rounded box; pills everywhere; purple or blue
gradients; huge padding around tiny content; a narrow centred column on a wide
screen; sparkles, wands, robots or any other "AI" motif; rows of unlabelled icons;
things that bounce, pulse or slide in on load; the untouched look of a component kit.
What good looks like: hierarchy made with type, spacing and alignment, not boxes;
hairlines over cards; dense but quiet; short, plain, specific labels; verbs on
buttons; numbers with units; waiting that shows real stages and real counts; focus
states that look designed; empty states that say what to do next.
Put a ten-line design rationale at the top of NOTES.md before you write code: the
idea, the grid, the type system, and what makes this not look generated.


8. HOW IT MUST BEHAVE AND PERFORM

- Split state by region, so a change in the device panel never redraws the library
  and the reverse. Keep fast-changing values (current time, device position, the
  playhead) out of React state: write them to the DOM directly from one
  requestAnimationFrame loop.
- Virtualise every list and grid with fixed row height and fixed tile aspect, with
  enough overscan that a jump never shows an empty list. Apply background results in
  batches without re-sorting the whole list. Search, sort and filter in memory.
- Keep the library's scroll position when a video, a panel or a filter is opened and
  closed.
- One visible <video> at a time, plus at most one hidden one preloading the next.
  Never a <video> per list item. Hover previews show a still for 4K-class sources.
- While a video plays: no layout work and no repaint outside the playhead, the
  motion dot and progress.
- Budgets (must hold with 21,000 items): usable library under 2 seconds; search
  results within 100 ms of a key; 60 frames a second while scrolling; a panel opens
  within 100 ms.
- Long calls (connecting takes 10 to 20 seconds, loading a script 1 to 5, scans much
  longer) show a running state with elapsed time and, where the app offers one, a
  cancel. Never invent a percentage the app does not report.
- Device commands are limited to about 240 a minute and take 1 to 2 seconds to take
  effect: send on release or at most every 400 to 500 ms while a control moves, show
  the chosen value at once and mark it "sending" until confirmed.

QUALITY BARS (outcomes; how you meet them is yours)
- Keyboard: everything is reachable without a mouse; focus order follows reading
  order; overlays trap focus and return it; Esc closes the top overlay; arrows move
  through the grid; Enter plays; Space toggles play; every device action shows its
  binding on its control.
- Gamepad: every action in the bindings table can be reached without the mouse; the
  focused control is always visible; focus states are large enough to see from a
  distance.
- Screen readers: every icon-only button has an aria-label; connection state and job
  updates are polite live regions; errors are assertive.
- Contrast at least 4.5:1 for all text, including the quietest. State is never shown
  by colour alone: confidence carries a word, device state an icon and a word, and the
  heatmap also encodes range by height.
- prefers-reduced-motion removes all motion except the playhead, the motion dot and
  progress.
- Built to stay fast with real data: 21,000 videos, 200+ tags, 100+ playlists.
  Fixed-height rows and fixed-aspect tiles so a virtualised list never jumps; no
  effect that forces the whole window to repaint while a video plays.
- Long titles truncate (two lines in tiles, middle ellipsis in rows), with the full
  text on hover or focus and in the detail panel.


