APPENDIX A. HOW THE APP WORKS UNDERNEATH (read from its source code)
Section numbers are those of the original engineering brief; 1, 12, 13 and 15 are
replaced by the sections above.

2. WINDOWS

Every window loads the same page; a query string picks what it shows.
  (none)          the main window: library, player, device panel
  ?popout=1       pop-out player: a bare video surface, no window frame
  ?library=1      the library alone
  ?deck=1         the device panel alone (kept above other windows)
  ?keys=1         keys and controller alone
  ?chat=1         assistant chat alone
  ?ai-popout=1    assistant controls alone
Windows do not share memory. They pass small messages through the main process:
  popout-command / popout-ready / popout-closed    main window <-> pop-out player
  library-bridge                                   main window <-> library, deck, keys, chat
  ai-control-bridge                                main window <-> assistant controls
Rule: the main window owns the truth (what is playing, the device connection). A
pop-out shows a copy of that state and sends commands back; it never talks to the
device itself. Design every pop-out so it is complete alone and shows "waiting for the
main window" until its first state message arrives.

There is also a phone/second-PC page served by the app on the local network (port
8787, protected by a PIN). It is the same interface running without Electron; device
panels are hidden there.


3. VIDEO: WHAT THE FILES ARE AND WHAT THE APP DOES WITH THEM

SyncPlayer does not compress, convert or re-encode video. It plays each file exactly
as it is on disk, in a standard HTML <video> element. What plays is whatever Chromium
128 can decode:
- Containers: MP4 / M4V / MOV, WebM, and MKV when the codecs inside are supported.
- Picture: H.264, HEVC (H.265, decoded on the graphics card), AV1, VP8, VP9.
- Sound: AAC, MP3, Opus, Vorbis, FLAC.
It cannot decode MPEG-2, Xvid/DivX, WMV/VC-1, ProRes, or AC-3 / E-AC-3 / DTS sound.

What happens when a file will not play
1. The <video> element raises an error (code 3 or 4).
2. The interface calls remuxVideo(path). The app repackages the file into MP4 without
   re-encoding (a container change only, no quality loss) and stores the copy in a
   cache folder. This takes seconds to a few minutes for a large file.
3. The interface plays the returned copy. If that fails too, it shows an error.
The interface must show these as real states: playing direct, getting ready (with a
progress message), playing the repackaged copy, cannot play (with the reason).
Planned, not built: converting sound only, and converting old codecs once on the
graphics card. Leave room for a "converting, 34%" state.

The library scanner lists these extensions only: mp4, mkv, avi, m4v, webm, mov, wmv.

Sizes and cost. Sources range from 480p to 4K and from a few megabytes to several
gigabytes. Decoding a 4K file is expensive, so the interface follows these rules, and
yours must too:
- One visible <video> at a time, plus at most one hidden one that preloads the next
  item so switching is instant (the current code calls this BufferedVideo: two decoder
  slots, the idle one muted and 1 pixel in size).
- Hover previews in the library play a second copy of the video ONLY for sources
  under 2560 wide and under 1440 high. For anything larger, show the cached still.
- Never create a <video> per list item. Never autoplay several previews at once.
- The scrub-bar hover preview seeks a hidden copy; it is switched off for 4K-class
  sources.

Stills. getThumbnail(path, second) returns one JPEG frame, 320 pixels wide, as a
data: URL. The app makes it with ffmpeg and caches it on disk. getThumbnailCached
returns the cached one or nothing, without doing any work. Load stills lazily, only
for items on screen.

Probing. Resolution and duration are read in the background (probeMediaBatch) and
cached. Until that finishes an item has width and height of 0 and a "pending" flag.
Tiles must not jump when the numbers arrive: fixed 16:9 boxes, fixed row heights.

File addresses. Local files are played through file:// addresses. Paths can contain
"#" and "?", which must be percent-encoded or the file will not load. Use the app's
existing helper for this; do not build addresses by hand.

Fit and fullscreen. The picture can be shown as fit, fill or stretched (one control
cycles them). Fullscreen uses the browser's fullscreen on the element that wraps the
video and its overlays, never on the <video> itself, so overlays stay visible.
Playback speed can change, but the device cannot follow any speed other than 1x: at
any other speed the device is paused and the interface must say so.

HDR. When Windows has HDR switched on for the monitor, Chromium shows HDR video in
HDR by itself and keeps the interface at normal brightness. The page can ask whether
the current screen is HDR with matchMedia('(dynamic-range: high)') and listen for
changes (the answer changes when the window moves to another monitor). Nothing in the
app can switch HDR on. Use the answer only to show a small "HDR" mark.

Screens. The owner uses three monitors at three scalings: 3840x2160 at 200%,
1920x1080 at 175%, 1280x720 at 100%. A window can be moved between them at any time.
In CSS pixels those screens are 1920x1080, 1097x617 and 1280x720, so a maximised
window on the second one is only about 1097x577. Design for a window from 1000x560 up
to 3840x2160, and never assume the scaling.


4. THE LIBRARY

Sources. The owner links folders (with or without sub-folders) and can also search a
whole drive for videos that already have a script. Several folders can be active at
once and are merged into one list.
  selectDirectory()                      -> a folder path, or nothing
  scanDirectories(dirs, recursive)       -> VideoStats[] for all of them
  scanDrive(root) + onScanDriveProgress  -> a long scan with progress messages
  watchDirectory(dirs, recursive) + onDirectoryChanged(dir)   the app tells the
                                            interface when files appear or disappear
  playbackFileExists(path)               -> true or false (a drive can be unplugged)

One item (VideoStats):
  videoPath      string   the file's full path. THIS IS THE ITEM'S IDENTITY. There is no
                          other id. Never show the raw path as a title.
  name           string   file name, shown as the title
  scriptPath     string?  the script file, if one exists
  hasFunscript   boolean
  hasNew         boolean? a second, newer script exists beside it
  durationMs     number
  strokes        number   how many strokes the script has
  spm            number   strokes per minute
  avgSpeed       number   average speed in units per second
  scriptedAt     number?  when the script was last written (for sorting)
  sizeBytes      number
  createdAt      number   file date
  addedAt        number?  when the app first saw the file
  folder         string?  which linked folder it came from
  width, height  number?  0 until the background probe fills them in
  contentKind    'pmv' | 'pov' | 'unknown'    read from the script's own metadata
  statsPending, probePending   boolean?       numbers still being worked out

How scripts are found. For clip.mp4 the default script is clip.funscript beside it.
Any file named clip.<label>.funscript beside it is another version of the script,
shown in a version switcher under the name <label>. listScriptVersions(videoPath)
returns [{ label, path }]. Switching version must not stop the video.

Size and speed. About 21,000 items. Sorting and grouping that list takes about 100 ms,
and background results arrive in batches of 50 to 100 items, each of which today
causes a full list pass. Requirements for the new interface:
- Virtualise every list and grid. Fixed row height and fixed tile aspect.
- Apply batch results without re-sorting the whole list on every batch.
- Keep scroll position when a panel, a filter or a video is opened and closed.
- Search, sort and filter in memory; never call the app per keystroke.

Sorting and filtering that exist today: by name, stroke speed, length, size, date
added, date scripted, rating; ascending or descending; "shows together, episodes in
order" grouping; filters for scripted or not, favourites, new, quality (resolution),
orientation, minimum rating, minimum and maximum length, tags (match all or any),
playlists. The search box also understands "ep 3", "ep 3+" and "ep 2-5".

Resume. getResumeAll() -> { [videoPath]: { t, dur, at } }; setResume(path, t, dur)
saves a position; a t under 5 seconds deletes the entry.

Show information. For videos that belong to a series the app can hold a title, a
description, a rating out of 10, genres and themes (getSeriesInfo, getSeriesIndex,
getSeriesCatalog). Treat every one of these texts as content: show it, never write
it yourself, and use placeholder text in mock-ups.


5. FUNSCRIPTS AND WHAT IS DRAWN FROM THEM

A funscript is JSON:
  { "version": "1.0", "inverted": false, "range": 100,
    "actions": [ { "at": 0, "pos": 10 }, { "at": 400, "pos": 90 }, ... ],
    "metadata": { ... } }
"at" is milliseconds from the start of the video. "pos" is 0 to 100: 0 is the base,
100 is the tip. The device moves in a straight line between consecutive points.
getFunscript(path) returns the parsed file.

Two numbers that are often confused. Speed is in units per second (how fast the
position changes). Stroke rate is in strokes per minute. Label each wherever shown.
The first-generation Handy starts shortening strokes somewhere between about 400 and
500 units per second and cannot move smoothly below about 30.

What the interface draws
- Heatmap: the whole script as a strip along the timeline, colour = speed, height =
  stroke range. It is drawn on a canvas, once per script, then only the playhead
  moves. It doubles as the scrub bar: click to seek, hover for the time.
- Live motion strip: the last few seconds of position as a line, with a dot for
  "now". It runs at 60 frames a second by interpolating between script points from
  the video's clock; it must not cause the rest of the window to redraw.
- Device bar: where the device should be right now (0 to 100).
Performance rule: while a video plays, only the playhead, the motion dot and progress
may animate. No effect may force the whole window to repaint (the current app's blur
and shadow effects are a suspected cause of stutter).


6. THE HANDY: HOW THE CONNECTION WORKS

The device is controlled through its maker's internet service. The app's main process
makes every call. The interface never contacts the service; it calls the methods
below and shows the state.

Connecting: connectHandy(key)
  The owner types a short connection key. The app then:
  1. asks the service whether a device with that key is online;
  2. checks which generation of the service the device speaks (newer firmware gets
     real pause and resume and a streaming mode; older gets the legacy calls);
  3. measures the clock difference between this PC and the service: 30 round trips,
     drops the 10 slowest, averages the rest.
  Step 3 alone takes about 12 seconds from Australia (each round trip is about 0.4 s).
  Returns { connected: true, api: 'v2' | 'v3', fw: string }
       or { connected: false, error: string }, for example "Handy device is not
       online" or "Handy API HTTP 500".
  The interface MUST show connecting as a state that lasts 10 to 20 seconds, with a
  moving indication, and must not let the owner press Connect twice. Today the button
  is disabled and reads "Connecting..." for the whole wait (measured 16 Nov: a second
  press does nothing), but nothing says how long it has been or how long it usually
  takes. Show the seconds elapsed and "usually 10 to 20 seconds". The app does not
  report stages, so do not invent a percentage.
  The key is a secret: show it masked unless the field has focus, never log it, never
  put it in a title or a tooltip.

Loading a script: uploadHandyScript(scriptPath, depth?, gap?)
  The app reads the script, applies whatever is switched on (depth scaling, gap fill,
  the stroke window), sends it to the service, and tells the device to load it.
  Returns { success: boolean, error?: string }. It takes 1 to 5 seconds. Every change
  that alters the script itself (a different version, a different fill pattern) means
  loading again, and the device pauses while that happens. Show "loading script"
  and debounce: wait about 700 ms after the last change before calling.

Playing in time
  playHandy(timeMs)      start the device at this position in the video
  pauseHandy()           stop the device (a true pause on newer firmware)
  syncHandyTime(timeMs)  the video jumped (a seek): move the device to match
  stopHandy()            stop movement but stay connected
  disconnectHandy()      stop and forget the connection
  The video element is the clock. The interface calls playHandy when the video starts
  playing, pauseHandy when it pauses or ends, syncHandyTime after every seek, and
  pauses the device whenever playback speed is not 1x. After a seek on a slow
  connection the device can be left in the wrong place, so always send the exact
  position again once the video is actually playing.
  setHandyOffset(ms) shifts the device earlier or later relative to the video
  (negative = earlier). The app supports it but TODAY'S INTERFACE HAS NO CONTROL FOR
  IT. Add one: a small stepper in milliseconds beside the connection state.

Limits that shape the controls
- The service accepts roughly 240 commands a minute per connection, shared by
  everything. Never send a command per pixel of a drag. Send when the control is
  released, or at most every 400 to 500 ms while it moves.
- A command takes 1 to 2 seconds to take effect. Controls must show the value the
  owner chose immediately and mark it "sending" until the app confirms.
- If the device drops off the network mid-play, the video keeps playing. Show the
  lost state and a Reconnect button; do not stop the video.

Live controls (no reload needed)
  liveTameSet({ pace, speed, length, vibe }, state)   four live sliders, 100 = as
      written. pace = how often it strokes, speed = how fast it travels, length =
      stroke length, vibe = an added shake (-1 = automatic, 0 = off, 1 to 3).
      When any slider leaves 100 the app switches to streaming points to the device
      ahead of time. A harder change lands within about 5 seconds; a gentler one
      restarts the stream with a hitch of about a second. Say so near the sliders.
  liveTameGet()                                       current values
  remoteSlide(min, max, mode, style)                  the stroke window: lowest and
      highest point (0 to 100). mode 'squash' uses the device's own range limit:
      instant, the shape is kept, everything gets slower. mode 'cap' means "keep the
      speed and tempo": the app rewrites the timing so strokes inside the window run
      at the script's own speed, and spare time is spent according to `style`
      (pause at the turn, soft landing, or a mix).
  setDeviceMotion({ rev, actions }) and setDevicePlayerState(state)   the interface
      tells the app what motion it is previewing, so the device and the on-screen
      preview show the same thing.

States the device area must be able to show
  not connected · connecting (10-20 s) · connected (with generation and firmware) ·
  loading script · playing · paused · paused because speed is not 1x · no script for
  this video · lost · error (with the message the app returned)


7. MOTION WITHOUT A SCRIPT, AND FILLING GAPS

Many videos have no script, and many scripts have long still sections. The app can
fill those with motion, and the owner steers it live. This is the densest part of the
current interface (87 controls in one pop-over) and the part most in need of order.

Pattern library. listPatterns() returns the hand-made pattern files, grouped by
folder (six folders, about 160 files). Each pattern is itself a short funscript.
readPattern(path), readAllPatterns() and readPatternsByPath() return their points.
patternsBrief() returns a compact list for the assistant. The pattern names describe
tempo and character; the app sorts them into tease, slow, mid, fast and finish from
those names. In mock-ups call them "Pattern set A", "Pattern 1".

The fill configuration is one object (GapCfg) that the interface edits and passes to
uploadHandyScript. Its main parts:
  on               fill is on or off
  ignoreScript     treat the whole video as one gap ("my motion instead")
  mode             how patterns are chosen: one fixed motion, shuffle, a saved
                   playlist of steps, or a "journey"
  pattern          a built-in motion or 'file:<path>' for a hand-made one
  mood             one of eight built-in moods
  shuffleFocus     words that narrow the shuffle to matching pattern names
  journey stages   each { focus, level, minutes }: for example slow start, build, finish
  journeyOrder     how stages are ordered; "fit to this video's length" scales the
                   build so the finish lands at the end
  scriptSpeed, scriptThin   how fast and how often the original script may stroke
  what counts as a gap: shortest gap in seconds, how still, and "anything slower than
                   this video's usual pace"
Settings are remembered per video and must never reset when the next video starts.
Turning fill off must always work, immediately.

Saved things. playlistsGet() / playlistsSet(all) read and write the saved pattern
playlists (ordered steps of pattern + seconds). Journeys are kept in the browser's
storage.

Preview. The interface can compute the next 30 seconds of motion from the
configuration and draw it as a line with the playhead, before anything is sent.

Keys and controller. Every motion action can be bound to a keyboard key and to a
gamepad button; sticks and triggers can drive live values (speed, the stroke window's
two ends). The gamepad is read with the browser's Gamepad API by polling. Rules
learnt the hard way, which the new interface must keep:
- stick input is linear: value x rate x time. No response curves, no dead-zone
  tricks, no "one axis at a time" rule.
- a change from a held stick is sent 400 ms after the stick settles;
- controller presses never pop up panels over the video.
Show the binding on each control it belongs to.

What the owner wants from manual mode: one place for it; presets on the pad (a speed
and a stroke length per button); hold to take over from the script and release to
hand back; a session that starts slow, builds and finishes, changing pattern every
20 to 30 seconds without repeating; a single 0-100 "how close" control that slows and
shortens the motion as it rises; pause buttons of 5 to 10 seconds; "stay on this
pattern".


8. PLAYLISTS, QUEUE, FAVOURITES, TAGS, HISTORY

All of these refer to videos by their full path (there is no stable id), so an item
whose file has moved shows as missing. Design a "missing" state for an item and never
delete it automatically.

  Queue            an ordered list of { name, videoPath, scriptPath, hasFunscript },
                   kept in the browser's storage. Play now, move up, move down, remove,
                   clear, save as a playlist.
  Saved playlists  named lists of the same items, kept in the browser's storage. Load
                   into the queue, show as a filter, combine several, edit in place.
  Favourites       a set of video paths in the browser's storage.
  My tags          named groups of videos kept in a file by the app:
                     myTagsGet()                         -> { tagName: paths[] }
                     myTagsSet({ name, paths, op })      op = 'add' | 'remove' | 'replace'
                     myTagsCombine({ names, newName })   union of several tags
                     myTagsTidy({ exclusive })           clean-up; the file is backed up first
                     myTagsMeta()                        dates and counts per tag
                   In today's interface "playlists" in the filter row are these tags.
  Library tags     genres and themes per series, from the show information. Filter by
                   any or all of the ticked tags.
  History          what was played, newest first.
Known weakness to design around: two different things are both called playlists
(saved queues in the browser, and "my tags" in a file). Present them as one concept
with one list; the owner should not need to know there are two stores.


9. WHERE SETTINGS LIVE

Files in the app's data folder, written only by the main process: the library cache,
resume positions, show information, my tags, pattern playlists, thumbnails, the
repackaged-video cache.
Browser storage (localStorage), written by the interface, all keys start with
"syncplayer_": active folders, sort and direction, view (grid or list), filters,
favourites, queue, playlists, UI size, theme, the fill configuration (global and per
video), the stroke window (global and per video, mode, style), key and controller
bindings, roulette settings, minimum rating.
Rules: keep every existing key and its meaning, so nothing the owner has set is lost.
New settings get new keys. Reading storage can fail; always fall back to a default.


